you see Dennis Quaid
but these kids in here,

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:42.810
they probably more readily
recognize you than Dennis Quaid.

00:33:42.810 --> 00:33:44.993
They probably don't even
know who Dennis Quaid is.

00:33:46.300 --> 00:33:50.788
Literally five seconds later
a group of four teenage girls

00:33:50.788 --> 00:33:54.816
walked by us and they were
saying, "That was Dennis Quaid."

00:33:54.816 --> 00:33:56.662
(both laughing)

00:33:56.662 --> 00:33:57.703
- Are you serious?

00:33:57.703 --> 00:33:59.656
(sighs happily)

00:33:59.656 --> 00:34:01.451
Really?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:34:01.451 --> 00:34:02.347
- Really?
- That was Dennis Quaid.

00:34:02.347 --> 00:34:05.010
- You know why, 'cause he was in the movie

00:34:05.010 --> 00:34:06.840
about the dolphin girl.

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:09.520
- Parent Trap.
- He was in Parent Trap.

00:34:09.520 --> 00:34:11.060
That was a remake of Parent Trap.

00:34:11.060 --> 00:34:12.260
- Dolphin girl's not Parent Trap,

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:14.068
I just saw Jacob held up Parent Trap.

00:34:14.068 --> 00:34:16.050
- What's the dolphin girl?

00:34:16.050 --> 00:34:19.100
- Free Willy?
(Link sputters)

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:21.886
- Not unless her leg was Willy.

00:34:21.886 --> 00:34:23.630
She got a leg bitten off.

00:34:23.630 --> 00:34:25.070
- By a dolphin?

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:26.730
- No by a shark.

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:30.380
- That's a, dolphin.
(Link laughing)

00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:31.730
You mean the girl that lost an arm?

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:33.820
- She lost her arm to a shark.

00:34:33.820 --> 00:34:34.688
- Soul Surfer or something like that.

00:34:34.688 --> 00:34:36.950
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, he
was the dad in that.

00:34:36.950 --> 00:34:39.800
- Yeah.
- That's how they know him.

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:40.930
No dolphins involved.

00:34:40.930 --> 00:34:43.480
- But they know his name,
you know what I'm saying?

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:45.890
People watch--
- And then,

00:34:45.890 --> 00:34:47.730
what about you though?

00:34:47.730 --> 00:34:51.030
- Well I will say that
then they did see me

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:53.748
and they did, it's not as good of a story

00:34:53.748 --> 00:34:57.710
when they recognized me but
they did also recognize me.

00:34:57.710 --> 00:35:00.970
- Was that after you asked
'em if they knew who you were?

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:02.963
- I did not approach them.

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:06.170
They saw me and then one
of the girls was like,

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:07.810
it's crazy, we're seeing
everybody in here,

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:09.300
or something like that.

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:14.300
So anyway, that Dennis Quaid, man.

00:35:14.320 --> 00:35:15.860
Shout-out to Dennis.

00:35:15.860 --> 00:35:17.470
If you wanna hang out at the dog park

00:35:17.470 --> 00:35:19.130
where you don't need a leash,

00:35:19.130 --> 00:35:21.250
the only appropriate place
where the dogs don't need

00:35:21.250 --> 00:35:22.630
to be on a leash, hit us up.

00:35:22.630 --> 00:35:25.440
- A lot of people, like I said at the top,

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:28.170
when they recognize me, like
last night we were walking

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:32.253
to meet friends including
you and Jessie at,

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:34.460
where were we, Highland Park.

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:35.530
- Yeah.
- Somebody comes around

00:35:35.530 --> 00:35:37.183
the corner like, hey man, let me,

00:35:38.518 --> 00:35:42.820
I know you, can I take
a little video with you?

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:44.160
And I'm like, oh, video.

00:35:44.160 --> 00:35:46.670
Ooh, I'm always like we
can just grab a selfie,

00:35:46.670 --> 00:35:47.550
but I didn't say that.

00:35:47.550 --> 00:35:49.905
- That's interesting 'cause I
got a video request as well.

00:35:49.905 --> 00:35:51.750
- He said video and usually they'll ask,

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:53.880
can you say so and so to this person?

00:35:53.880 --> 00:35:56.170
But instead he just
held it up like a selfie

00:35:56.170 --> 00:35:59.650
as if it was a photo but he
was doing video and I waved.

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:02.870
I was like uh, that's actually
never happened before.

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:06.287
And then he said, "Does
this happen a lot?"

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:10.410
So whenever I'm recognized,
I do get that follow-up.

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:13.310
I do think that like you
said, people wanna know

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:14.840
if they're the only one that does this

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:16.810
or if they're maybe I guess,

00:36:16.810 --> 00:36:19.110
am I being annoying because
this has happened five times

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:20.360
as you rounded the block.

00:36:21.610 --> 00:36:23.560
So I guess I'll answer
that question a little bit

00:36:23.560 --> 00:36:24.393
in the middle.

00:36:24.393 --> 00:36:27.890
I think it does happen a lot,

00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:32.890
and basically, every
single time I go out now,

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:35.833
it's multiple times.

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:40.510
I had to run errands
with the kids and I went

00:36:40.510 --> 00:36:44.640
to the grocery store and
then the drug store next door

00:36:44.640 --> 00:36:47.490
and then we got in the
car and drove a block away

00:36:47.490 --> 00:36:49.830
and went to this poke bowl place.

00:36:49.830 --> 00:36:54.490
I walked in the grocery
store and I got recognized.

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:59.040
And then I was checking
out at the drug store

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:01.070
and I got recognized.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:04.080
And then we went to the poke bowl place

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:08.760
and the guy working
there said something else

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:11.340
that I've started to get more frequently

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:16.327
and that is the question,
"Has anyone ever told you

00:37:16.327 --> 00:37:20.210
"that you look a lot like
a guy from the internet?"

00:37:20.210 --> 00:37:21.280
- That happens quite a bit.

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:26.280
- And so what I say to
that is, "I do get that.

00:37:26.287 --> 00:37:28.520
"A lot actually."

00:37:28.520 --> 00:37:31.160
And the reason why I
say that is 'cause like,

00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:33.810
one of my kids is usually with
me and I like messin' around

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:36.840
with people and making them feel like,

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:40.463
and Lily was like, Dad. (sighs)

00:37:42.043 --> 00:37:44.810
This is not a heist, no she
said that for the other one.

00:37:44.810 --> 00:37:47.947
For this thing she said, "It is him.

00:37:47.947 --> 00:37:50.380
"He likes to mess with people."

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:52.210
Because when we were, I
think I told this story,

00:37:52.210 --> 00:37:54.420
when we were in London at
the Harry Potter thing,

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:58.602
these kids were like, excuse me, sir.

00:37:58.602 --> 00:37:59.930
Have you ever--

00:37:59.930 --> 00:38:01.540
- Tiny Tim.
- Have you ever been told

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:02.528
that you look like--

00:38:02.528 --> 00:38:03.393
- They all sound like Tiny Tim.

00:38:03.393 --> 00:38:05.410
- You look like someone from the internet.

00:38:05.410 --> 00:38:07.433
I'm like yes, exactly.

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:11.910
I get it a lot that I
look exactly like someone

00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:14.187
from the internet, exactly,
and I kept saying that

00:38:14.187 --> 00:38:16.360
and they nodded their
heads and walked off.

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:18.060
Then they came back later and got pictures

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:20.300
because I was in a confined
environment where we were all

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:21.575
going through a tour together.

00:38:21.575 --> 00:38:23.503
(sighs) I mean, after,

00:38:25.067 --> 00:38:26.770
I should just say that

00:38:28.380 --> 00:38:33.110
it's frickin' surreal to
find yourself walking around

00:38:33.110 --> 00:38:35.823
and just, it's happening,

00:38:36.750 --> 00:38:38.910
I don't know if we're the
most popular we ever been

00:38:38.910 --> 00:38:40.440
but it's happening more now than ever.

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:42.520
Like it continues to happen more and more.

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:44.000
- Right.
- I think it's the nature

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:46.970
of just the popularity of the medium.

00:38:46.970 --> 00:38:48.320
Like more and more people are watching,

00:38:48.320 --> 00:38:50.450
even if you don't watch our videos,

00:38:50.450 --> 00:38:53.100
you can't escape our thumbnails.

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:56.650
So I think, and that brings me to a theory

00:38:56.650 --> 00:38:58.280
about something else which
I'm sure we'll get into

00:38:58.280 --> 00:38:59.910
about what you hear people saying a lot,

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:03.280
but I don't know if we're the
most popular we've ever been

00:39:03.280 --> 00:39:05.370
but we're the most visible.

00:39:05.370 --> 00:39:07.516
And I guess maybe they're the same thing.

00:39:07.516 --> 00:39:10.000
So it's happening a lot
and I just wanna say

00:39:11.090 --> 00:39:14.032
I'm extremely grateful
because it's an indicator--

00:39:14.032 --> 00:39:15.960
- Right.
- Of things working,

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:20.960
like oh, we create our stuff to be seen

00:39:21.302 --> 00:39:25.159
and so when people see it
and we get recognized more,

00:39:25.159 --> 00:39:27.760
that's like that's a good
sign, oh this is working.

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:32.760
It's also freakin' surreal
because in my innermost being,

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:36.640
I have a hard time
actually thinking of myself

00:39:36.640 --> 00:39:37.973
as a famous person.

00:39:39.260 --> 00:39:42.010
It's like I don't think of
myself as an adult either

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:44.900
a lot of times, I mean, I
know how my brain works.

00:39:44.900 --> 00:39:47.922
I know the thoughts and feelings
and processes I go through

00:39:47.922 --> 00:39:49.487
and I feel like I might as well be

00:39:49.487 --> 00:39:52.130
in fourth grade a lot of times, you know?

00:39:52.130 --> 00:39:54.270
- I can attest to that.
- And in a similar way,

00:39:54.270 --> 00:39:58.080
I feel like I know who famous people are.

00:39:58.080 --> 00:40:03.080
I mean, I've seen Parent Trap,
the remake with Dennis Quaid.

00:40:07.680 --> 00:40:11.240
But I just don't feel like
I'm one of those people

00:40:11.240 --> 00:40:13.083
but you know what, I guess I am

00:40:13.083 --> 00:40:14.790
because I get recognized everywhere I go.

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:17.780
- And I think there's a few dynamics.

00:40:17.780 --> 00:40:21.783
One is, like you said, it's an indicator,

00:40:23.320 --> 00:40:25.610
so be grateful.

00:40:25.610 --> 00:40:29.847
It can be a little bit
of a nuisance, at times.

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:37.010
Now in the moment I never,
I wanna make this clear,

00:40:37.010 --> 00:40:40.488
in the moment, unless somebody
says or does something

00:40:40.488 --> 00:40:44.063
that is particularly annoying
which we'll get into,

00:40:44.984 --> 00:40:48.530
I appreciate it and there's
nothing against an individual

00:40:48.530 --> 00:40:50.250
who comes up and recognizes me.

00:40:50.250 --> 00:40:52.860
But it's sometimes going
into the situation,

00:40:52.860 --> 00:40:55.910
knowing that it could happen,
that's what's annoying,

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:58.060
not the person who did
it but just the dynamic

00:40:58.060 --> 00:40:59.280
of the fact that it could happen.

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:00.313
For instance,

00:41:04.030 --> 00:41:05.730
we were coming back from someplace

00:41:06.580 --> 00:41:10.317
and I was like I'd like to make some,

00:41:11.293 --> 00:41:12.550
I can't remember what it was,

00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:14.270
I think I wanted to make some burgers

00:41:14.270 --> 00:41:15.660
and I'd watch the YouTube video

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:18.100
about the perfect skillet
burger or whatever

00:41:18.100 --> 00:41:19.750
and I was like I'm
gonna make these burgers

00:41:19.750 --> 00:41:21.940
with this really nice beef or whatever

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:23.810
and Jessie's like okay
well I don't have any

00:41:23.810 --> 00:41:26.810
of that stuff so let's just
go by the grocery store.

00:41:26.810 --> 00:41:29.310
And we pull up to the
grocery store and I'm like,

00:41:29.310 --> 00:41:31.090
can you go?

00:41:31.090 --> 00:41:33.540
She was super tired, she was
like, I don't wanna go in.

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:35.010
If you wanna cook this stuff, you go in.

00:41:35.010 --> 00:41:37.670
I was like, I don't wanna
go in because I just,

00:41:37.670 --> 00:41:39.090
I really don't wanna be
recognized right now.

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:41.723
It wasn't like I was having
a bad hair day or something.

00:41:42.784 --> 00:41:46.288
Sometimes I get into
the grocery store and--

00:41:46.288 --> 00:41:47.180
- Well you have to be--
- I have to have

00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:48.600
multiple interactions.

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:52.093
- Yeah and they have
to be for that person.

00:41:53.222 --> 00:41:55.560
Every interaction you
have is for that person,

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:57.136
it's not for you.

00:41:57.136 --> 00:42:00.750
I don't go into the grocery store unless

00:42:00.750 --> 00:42:03.569
I'm taking someone I
wanna impress I guess.

00:42:03.569 --> 00:42:07.320
I don't go into these
places saying I wanna stop

00:42:07.320 --> 00:42:08.880
and have interactions.
- Yeah yeah yeah.

00:42:08.880 --> 00:42:11.927
- That are for that person
to make them feel special.

00:42:11.927 --> 00:42:12.760
- Sometimes you might be in that mood

00:42:12.760 --> 00:42:14.350
which I wanna talk about
that, but sometimes you're

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:16.160
in the I don't wanna get recognized mood

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:17.850
because I just wanna get in and get out--

00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:19.660
- No one would win.

00:42:19.660 --> 00:42:22.600
- Because I want the
interaction to be something

00:42:22.600 --> 00:42:24.690
that is positive and
memorable to this person

00:42:24.690 --> 00:42:28.320
'cause I do care about
anyone who watches what we do

00:42:28.320 --> 00:42:31.430
enough to know who we are,
I care about that person.

00:42:31.430 --> 00:42:35.754
So I don't wanna be an, to
this person even if I'm feeling

00:42:35.754 --> 00:42:38.173
particularly like an, at the time.

00:42:39.970 --> 00:42:42.200
So Jessie was like you're
not gonna get recognized

00:42:42.200 --> 00:42:43.736
at the grocery store.

00:42:43.736 --> 00:42:46.010
She was basically trying to manipulate me

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:47.260
so I would be the one to go in.

00:42:47.260 --> 00:42:49.610
'Cause she had her feet up on the dash.

00:42:49.610 --> 00:42:51.576
- Oh she did.
- Speaking of dirty dashes.

00:42:51.576 --> 00:42:53.210
(Rhett laughs)
- I know a guy--

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:54.870
- I need to call Dr. Noodle to buff out

00:42:54.870 --> 00:42:58.010
those Doc Marten stains. (chuckles)

00:42:58.010 --> 00:43:01.893
But so, not a sponsor.

00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:04.760
I don't know why I felt
the need to say what brand

00:43:04.760 --> 00:43:06.430
of shoes my wife had on at the time.

00:43:06.430 --> 00:43:08.580
- I thought you were saying
Dr. Noodle's not a sponsor.

00:43:08.580 --> 00:43:09.413
But he will be.

00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:13.863
- So Locke and I go in together.

00:43:15.490 --> 00:43:20.459
Literally I walk in

00:43:20.459 --> 00:43:22.120
to the grocery store

00:43:22.120 --> 00:43:27.120
and I'm like where are the,
what do you call the things?

00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:28.910
Carrying things.

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:30.410
- [Both] Baskets.

00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:32.350
- I was like where are the baskets at,

00:43:32.350 --> 00:43:35.083
and a guy was like oh, right over here.

00:43:36.831 --> 00:43:38.870
You from Good Mythical Morning? (chuckles)

00:43:38.870 --> 00:43:42.390
It was literally the
first guy we talked to

00:43:42.390 --> 00:43:43.830
and Locke just got a kick
out of it 'cause he was like

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:45.260
Mom said you wouldn't get recognized

00:43:45.260 --> 00:43:49.110
and then it happened two more
times in the seven minutes

00:43:49.110 --> 00:43:51.770
that I was getting the three
ingredients that I needed.

00:43:51.770 --> 00:43:54.118
And again it's not like it
was some big inconvenience

00:43:54.118 --> 00:43:55.990
but it was just like,
again, you feel like,

00:43:55.990 --> 00:43:56.968
if this person has a question,

00:43:56.968 --> 00:43:59.993
I wanna give them the time that,

00:44:01.371 --> 00:44:02.770
this is a one chance

00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:06.230
and they wanna have an
interaction that's special

00:44:06.230 --> 00:44:09.763
or whatever and so but I just
wanna get burgers and go.

00:44:10.840 --> 00:44:12.830
But then there's the
opposite end of the spectrum

00:44:12.830 --> 00:44:15.370
which is I remember
one time our friend Gar

00:44:16.900 --> 00:44:19.352
who we met Tony Hale through

00:44:19.352 --> 00:44:24.136
and obviously Tony at this
point is like a huge star

00:44:24.136 --> 00:44:27.823
and would be recognized
basically anywhere that he went,

00:44:29.726 --> 00:44:32.090
at the time he was pretty much known

00:44:32.090 --> 00:44:34.403
for Arrested Development.
- Yeah.

00:44:34.403 --> 00:44:36.990
Veep had not started.
- Veep had not started,

00:44:36.990 --> 00:44:39.960
yeah and he wasn't gonna be
the frickin' main character

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:41.563
in the upcoming Toy Story.

00:44:44.303 --> 00:44:45.730
- Were you up for that role or something?

00:44:45.730 --> 00:44:48.290
Why'd you get angry?
(Rhett laughs)

00:44:48.290 --> 00:44:50.670
My friend got more famous, gah!

00:44:50.670 --> 00:44:51.813
- I'm not angry, no.

00:44:53.280 --> 00:44:57.270
But in fact, if anybody should get

00:44:57.270 --> 00:45:00.050
what he's gotten, it's him.

00:45:00.050 --> 00:45:01.766
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:45:01.766 --> 00:45:04.500
- But Gar said, you know,

00:45:04.500 --> 00:45:07.280
he was joking 'cause Gar
has this way of finding

00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:11.720
this thing out about you or
assuming something about you

00:45:11.720 --> 00:45:13.430
in a way that it's just funny, right.

00:45:13.430 --> 00:45:15.261
- Yeah yeah.
- So he was like,

00:45:15.261 --> 00:45:19.140
you know, whenever Tony needs
a self-confidence boost,

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:22.190
he just goes to the Apple Store. (laughs)

00:45:22.190 --> 00:45:23.460
- 'Cause they're Arrested Development.

00:45:23.460 --> 00:45:27.841
- Right because the
people who work at Apple

00:45:27.841 --> 00:45:31.510
and/or shop at Apple at the time,

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:33.680
Gar's joke was that there
was a lot of crossover,

00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:35.550
those people were the
Arrested Development crowd

00:45:35.550 --> 00:45:36.740
and they would recognize him.

00:45:36.740 --> 00:45:39.711
Now he didn't actually go to the,

00:45:39.711 --> 00:45:41.965
he went to the Apple
Store to get a product,

00:45:41.965 --> 00:45:45.640
but that was Gar's little
joke is he was like

00:45:45.640 --> 00:45:47.100
anytime he's been with
him in an Apple Store,

00:45:47.100 --> 00:45:48.680
he's gotten recognized so he must do this

00:45:48.680 --> 00:45:49.720
for self-confidence boosts.

00:45:49.720 --> 00:45:51.900
- Whenever we wanna get
a self-confidence boost,

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:53.900
we go to the meat section in a Ralphs.

00:45:53.900 --> 00:45:55.369
- (chuckles) That's right.

00:45:55.369 --> 00:45:57.240
That's where the Mythical Beasts hang out.

00:45:57.240 --> 00:46:00.157
- No I was making a
statement more about us

00:46:00.157 --> 00:46:04.017
and our proclivity to
eat butchered refuse.

00:46:06.020 --> 00:46:07.210
- But I will say--

00:46:07.210 --> 00:46:09.010
- So the back of the butcher counter.

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:10.210
The stuff they don't put out front.

00:46:10.210 --> 00:46:11.830
- Those are the two dynamics, right.

00:46:11.830 --> 00:46:14.740
There's the I'm in a situation right now

00:46:14.740 --> 00:46:16.720
where I'm at the airport
and I'm trying to move

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:19.240
to a location or I'm at an amusement park

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:22.330
and I don't want to suddenly
have to get a picture

00:46:22.330 --> 00:46:24.830
with somebody 'cause that
might lead to this other person

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:26.660
wanting a picture and this
other person wanting a picture.

00:46:26.660 --> 00:46:28.780
There are situations like that where

00:46:28.780 --> 00:46:30.710
it seems like an inconvenience
and then there are times

00:46:30.710 --> 00:46:32.470
where it's just like, you know what,

00:46:32.470 --> 00:46:34.880
and this is never something that you think

00:46:35.720 --> 00:46:38.330
but it's just like oh I'm
with these people right now,

00:46:38.330 --> 00:46:40.536
or I'm in this place right now.

00:46:40.536 --> 00:46:43.170
I'm in this place that
I think the people here

00:46:43.170 --> 00:46:45.110
are particularly cool or something.

00:46:45.110 --> 00:46:47.567
I have some reason to think
that these people have

00:46:47.567 --> 00:46:50.448
a particular taste in comedy,

00:46:50.448 --> 00:46:53.050
boy it'd be cool to get recognized here.

00:46:53.050 --> 00:46:56.520
Again, this isn't an actual
conscious thought process

00:46:56.520 --> 00:46:58.900
but this is sort of the
emotional undercurrent

00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:01.420
that is happening from time to time.

00:47:01.420 --> 00:47:02.350
'Cause you remember when we were--

00:47:02.350 --> 00:47:04.400
- There are times when
we wanna be recognized

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:07.870
like I'm in this, I'm
in Melbourne, Australia,

00:47:07.870 --> 00:47:09.810
and I think this is one
of the coolest places

00:47:09.810 --> 00:47:11.720
I've ever been and then if I feel like I'm

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:13.270
in one of the coolest
places I've ever been,

00:47:13.270 --> 00:47:18.130
if somebody with a cool
backpack meets me on the bridge

00:47:18.130 --> 00:47:20.921
going over the river and I get recognized,

00:47:20.921 --> 00:47:23.620
even if they don't know that
we have a show that night,

00:47:23.620 --> 00:47:25.440
I can get over that because I'm like,

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:26.760
oh that was a cool backpack.

00:47:26.760 --> 00:47:30.330
I really, I like that
person's taste in backpacks.

00:47:30.330 --> 00:47:34.880
And they like my show so hmm,
there's a correlation there.

00:47:34.880 --> 00:47:36.310
- I'm cool. (laughs)

00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:38.070
Right.
- Therefore, I am cool.

00:47:38.070 --> 00:47:38.910
- Right.
- Well you remember,

00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:43.170
so we were in New York and
we were lower east side

00:47:43.170 --> 00:47:44.820
or something, we're walking along

00:47:46.410 --> 00:47:51.410
and there are six to eight
guys sitting on this stoop

00:47:51.950 --> 00:47:56.950
and basically like it feels
like a television show,

00:47:57.420 --> 00:47:59.140
it's like this is a scene from a movie,

00:47:59.140 --> 00:48:00.809
this isn't normal, this isn't--

00:48:00.809 --> 00:48:05.610
- It was nighttime and it
was a bit seedier than,

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:08.190
this wasn't network TV, this was like,

00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:12.190
this could be a scene
in an HBO seedy movie.

00:48:12.190 --> 00:48:13.795
- Like The Wire.
- Yeah.

00:48:13.795 --> 00:48:16.570
- But it wasn't Baltimore,
it was New York.

00:48:16.570 --> 00:48:18.890
And so you got these guys
kinda sitting on this stoop--

00:48:18.890 --> 00:48:20.520
- Not seedy but gritty.

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:21.403
Let's say gritty.

00:48:22.380 --> 00:48:26.300
- And some guys who were sort
of embodying the grittiness

00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:29.313
of the area, shall we say.
- Right.

00:48:30.550 --> 00:48:33.270
We were fish out of water walking by.

00:48:33.270 --> 00:48:36.170
- We walk by, we actually get past them,

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:39.220
and all of a sudden, a guy's like hold up!

00:48:39.220 --> 00:48:41.530
- We might have even sped up, honestly.

00:48:41.530 --> 00:48:43.080
- Rhett and Link?

00:48:43.080 --> 00:48:44.850
And I'm like what?

00:48:44.850 --> 00:48:49.837
And then a guy who I'm just describing him

00:48:50.850 --> 00:48:55.850
because he's actually known
on the internet for this.

00:48:56.720 --> 00:49:01.720
He calls himself T-Rex, I
think he's got an Instagram.

00:49:03.990 --> 00:49:07.060
But he does not have arms, he has hands.

00:49:07.060 --> 00:49:09.790
He just has hands and no arms.

00:49:09.790 --> 00:49:14.240
And he wears this as a badge of honor.

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:15.790
He didn't have a shirt on.

00:49:15.790 --> 00:49:18.450
It's his thing, he has fully embraced it.

00:49:18.450 --> 00:49:19.283
- Yeah.

00:49:19.283 --> 00:49:21.670
- And so he comes up to us and he's like,

00:49:21.670 --> 00:49:23.370
I'm a big fan.

00:49:23.370 --> 00:49:25.330
And he's tellin' the other
guys and another guy's like,

00:49:25.330 --> 00:49:26.730
yeah I know who they are or whatever.

00:49:26.730 --> 00:49:28.820
And then I was blown away,

00:49:28.820 --> 00:49:32.170
I was like this interesting
combination of the coolest guys

00:49:32.170 --> 00:49:36.180
who've ever recognized us
are saying that they're fans

00:49:36.180 --> 00:49:40.400
and then he explained
who he was and he puts

00:49:40.400 --> 00:49:45.361
on a T-Rex head and goes
around and does T-Rex motions.

00:49:45.361 --> 00:49:46.194
(Link chuckles)

00:49:46.194 --> 00:49:47.240
- Yeah it was wild, it was wild.

00:49:47.240 --> 00:49:48.740
- But that was this thing that

00:49:49.810 --> 00:49:51.222
we talk about that all the time

00:49:51.222 --> 00:49:53.530
because it was just like those guys watch.

00:49:53.530 --> 00:49:56.740
- Yeah we're in each
other's orbits, it's weird.

00:49:56.740 --> 00:49:58.500
- And 'cause--
- In a cool way.

00:49:58.500 --> 00:50:02.770
- Again, I feel like I
wanna constantly apologize

00:50:02.770 --> 00:50:05.830
for, we're just trying to
be honest about the way

00:50:05.830 --> 00:50:08.040
our thoughts work, it's like
I'm not trying to be like

00:50:08.040 --> 00:50:10.340
oh look we get recognized by cool people.

00:50:10.340 --> 00:50:14.677
I'm trying to explain the
psyche of what it does to you

00:50:14.677 --> 00:50:16.713
and what it could do to us.

00:50:17.950 --> 00:50:20.020
I could easily see how
this could become something

00:50:20.020 --> 00:50:22.710
that you sort of rely on like
an injection into your arm.

00:50:22.710 --> 00:50:24.130
You know what I'm saying?

00:50:24.130 --> 00:50:28.460
- So I think a healthier response is to,

00:50:28.460 --> 00:50:29.830
if I'm gonna err on the side of something,

00:50:29.830 --> 00:50:33.819
it's being over it versus desiring it,

00:50:33.819 --> 00:50:38.480
so I guess it's a good
sign that the struggle

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:40.660
that we have is being gracious

00:50:40.660 --> 00:50:42.960
and continuing to be grateful.

00:50:42.960 --> 00:50:47.270
So being gracious to the
person no matter what they say,

00:50:47.270 --> 00:50:50.810
if it's awkward or if it's
unintentionally insulting.

00:50:50.810 --> 00:50:52.070
- Well yeah.
- Which happens a lot.

00:50:52.070 --> 00:50:53.360
- That's a whole category we can get into.

00:50:53.360 --> 00:50:56.350
- That's a whole category,
but then switching to

00:50:57.350 --> 00:50:59.510
being gracious, like
you said, and grateful.

00:50:59.510 --> 00:51:01.960
So how we feel about it.

00:51:01.960 --> 00:51:06.710
And so we can land there
in a few minutes I think

00:51:06.710 --> 00:51:10.320
and also talk about
boundaries 'cause I'm starting

00:51:10.320 --> 00:51:11.910
to think about that and I have one story

00:51:11.910 --> 00:51:13.740
but before we do, I do think that

00:51:17.512 --> 00:51:19.180
there are unintentionally
insulting things,

00:51:19.180 --> 00:51:21.810
I think we've talked about these
before and it's just funny.

00:51:21.810 --> 00:51:25.719
It's not that we're
actually, well I don't know.

00:51:25.719 --> 00:51:27.043
Maybe go through and then we'll see if--

00:51:27.043 --> 00:51:28.190
- There's one situation where--

00:51:28.190 --> 00:51:30.850
- If it actually hurts or
if it's just kinda funny

00:51:30.850 --> 00:51:31.685
and that's why we're talking about it.

00:51:31.685 --> 00:51:33.510
- I told the story before
about the one time I was

00:51:33.510 --> 00:51:35.080
at the airport and we were panicking,

00:51:35.080 --> 00:51:36.387
we had missed a flight
and somebody came up

00:51:36.387 --> 00:51:39.460
and I think I was probably
as short as I have ever been

00:51:39.460 --> 00:51:40.760
with somebody but...

00:51:44.200 --> 00:51:46.993
I don't think I'm
exaggerating, 70% of the time,

00:51:48.240 --> 00:51:49.690
people say one of two things.

00:51:51.340 --> 00:51:54.120
One is I used to watch you all the time.

00:51:54.120 --> 00:51:55.880
- Right.
- And the second thing is

00:51:55.880 --> 00:52:00.070
my fill in the blank, cousin, sister, kid,

00:52:00.070 --> 00:52:02.930
mom, brother, is the biggest fan.

00:52:02.930 --> 00:52:04.900
- Somebody besides me.
- Somebody besides the person

00:52:04.900 --> 00:52:06.334
who's coming up to you.

00:52:06.334 --> 00:52:10.483
So 70, and this is just
anecdotal but 70% of the time,

00:52:11.553 --> 00:52:15.550
somebody says something
that basically says that,

00:52:15.550 --> 00:52:17.610
me currently, the one coming up to you,

00:52:17.610 --> 00:52:19.120
I'm not a fan, I don't care. (chuckles)

00:52:19.120 --> 00:52:21.242
I used to care or somebody else cares.

00:52:21.242 --> 00:52:22.890
- Right.
- And so that's--

00:52:22.890 --> 00:52:25.680
- So I'm bothering you to tell you

00:52:25.680 --> 00:52:29.800
that I'm not really into you
is the worst interpretation.

00:52:29.800 --> 00:52:31.440
- And basically what I say every time is,

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:33.830
if somebody says my so
and so is a big fan,

00:52:33.830 --> 00:52:35.670
I'm like, you're not?

00:52:35.670 --> 00:52:36.840
That's what I say.

00:52:36.840 --> 00:52:38.027
And then if somebody says I used to watch,

00:52:38.027 --> 00:52:39.253
I'm like why'd you stop?

00:52:40.300 --> 00:52:42.910
Because I mean, I'm not being a jerk,

00:52:42.910 --> 00:52:44.910
I'm just legitimately like okay.

00:52:44.910 --> 00:52:47.190
- Well it's a little jerkish.

00:52:47.190 --> 00:52:48.710
They say I used to watch, I'll say,

00:52:48.710 --> 00:52:53.070
like the guy in the poke
bowl place who I had to wait

00:52:53.070 --> 00:52:54.597
for the order so we
ended up talking to him

00:52:54.597 --> 00:52:56.910
for like eight minutes.

00:52:56.910 --> 00:52:59.450
But the kick-off was I used to watch you.

00:52:59.450 --> 00:53:01.953
After he said, "Has anyone
told you you look like

00:53:01.953 --> 00:53:04.000
"the guy from the internet
that I used to watch,"

00:53:04.000 --> 00:53:05.650
I guess, you put it all together.

00:53:06.560 --> 00:53:09.370
And I'm like well we're
still doing it man.

00:53:09.370 --> 00:53:11.270
And that's when it starts to feel like

00:53:12.770 --> 00:53:15.840
we're getting recognized more
but we got all these people

00:53:15.840 --> 00:53:18.320
who are recognizing us
for not watching anymore.

00:53:18.320 --> 00:53:19.720
What does that mean, we're falling off

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:23.030
and it plays into my
insecurities, I'm like ugh.

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:26.040
So that's not healthy.

00:53:27.233 --> 00:53:30.090
- Well, first of all, I think

00:53:31.050 --> 00:53:35.113
if you're talking about
our emotional state,

00:53:36.300 --> 00:53:37.808
this is good right?

00:53:37.808 --> 00:53:38.790
This is good.

00:53:38.790 --> 00:53:41.320
I don't want to be somebody
who is constantly like--

00:53:41.320 --> 00:53:43.364
- Adulated.
- Adulated and built up

00:53:43.364 --> 00:53:45.410
by other people 'cause A,

00:53:45.410 --> 00:53:47.330
it makes me feel a little uncomfortable,

00:53:47.330 --> 00:53:51.530
but also it turns you into a jerk.

00:53:51.530 --> 00:53:54.430
It's very difficult if you
are just a normal person.

00:53:54.430 --> 00:53:56.979
Even if you're a normal,
well-adjusted person who,

00:53:56.979 --> 00:54:01.520
this all happened to us
after we were fully adults

00:54:01.520 --> 00:54:04.510
with kids, married, we got
lots of things in our lives

00:54:04.510 --> 00:54:08.040
that ground us, but even
with all those things

00:54:08.040 --> 00:54:12.230
in place, if suddenly
everyone cares about you

00:54:12.230 --> 00:54:14.770
because of something you did
and they wanna be around you

00:54:14.770 --> 00:54:17.520
or they, that can create problems.

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:20.180
You can become a person that
no one wants to be around

00:54:20.180 --> 00:54:22.140
so we are constantly
guarding against that,

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:26.500
but it helps when 70% of the
time people recognize you,

00:54:26.500 --> 00:54:28.090
they're kind of insulting you.

00:54:28.090 --> 00:54:28.923
- Well--

00:54:28.923 --> 00:54:31.133
- It kinda balances things out.

00:54:31.133 --> 00:54:32.910
- Obviously they're not
intentionally insulting us

00:54:32.910 --> 00:54:35.140
and I think it's an insecurity that leads

00:54:35.140 --> 00:54:36.500
to it being interpreted as an insult

00:54:36.500 --> 00:54:39.580
but it's also a good thing,
it's also a position indicator

00:54:39.580 --> 00:54:42.875
that our work is getting out there

00:54:42.875 --> 00:54:44.740
and again, I wanna talk about our work

00:54:44.740 --> 00:54:47.900
and not talk about us because
that's what I want this

00:54:47.900 --> 00:54:49.620
to be about, it's the stuff that we create

00:54:49.620 --> 00:54:52.120
even if we're the center
of the show or whatever.

00:54:52.120 --> 00:54:54.960
It's a sign that it's getting
out there to the point

00:54:54.960 --> 00:54:57.560
where even if you don't
watch it, you know about it.

00:54:58.760 --> 00:55:02.970
People say millions in marketing
for just that to happen.

00:55:02.970 --> 00:55:05.253
Awareness, not even enjoyment.

00:55:05.253 --> 00:55:08.290
- Yeah.
- Awareness is arguably

00:55:08.290 --> 00:55:10.460
just as powerful, it's
like I've got an opinion

00:55:10.460 --> 00:55:13.421
about something that I
don't even actively watch.

00:55:13.421 --> 00:55:16.470
- I think that's evidenced
by, so you're kinda talking

00:55:16.470 --> 00:55:18.410
about people who--
- That's a good sign.

00:55:18.410 --> 00:55:20.852
- Aren't familiar and this is something

00:55:20.852 --> 00:55:25.270
that has happened
repeatedly that demonstrates

00:55:25.270 --> 00:55:26.820
what you're talking about
is definitely the case

00:55:26.820 --> 00:55:29.470
that people just kind of know.

00:55:29.470 --> 00:55:31.370
A, they don't know the name of the show.

00:55:31.370 --> 00:55:34.119
B, they don't know our names.

00:55:34.119 --> 00:55:38.070
But the most interesting and
funniest thing that's happened

00:55:38.070 --> 00:55:40.348
is I'll be with someone who is not you.

00:55:40.348 --> 00:55:41.813
(Link chuckles)
Okay.

00:55:42.720 --> 00:55:44.280
This most recently
happened with my brother,

00:55:44.280 --> 00:55:46.240
same trip when he was in town.

00:55:46.240 --> 00:55:49.770
And somebody came up and
they were like, ah, oh, uh,

00:55:49.770 --> 00:55:51.343
you're that guy.

00:55:52.730 --> 00:55:55.570
You're that guy from the internet.

00:55:55.570 --> 00:55:58.190
And I helped him, I was like
yeah Good Mythical Morning.

00:55:58.190 --> 00:55:59.450
They're like yeah yeah yeah yeah yeah.

00:55:59.450 --> 00:56:02.253
And then my brother's
with me and they're like,

00:56:03.757 --> 00:56:05.293
and you're the other guy.

00:56:06.977 --> 00:56:10.170
And my brother was like yeah!
(both chuckle)

00:56:10.170 --> 00:56:11.790
- 'Cause he's your brother
and that's a thing that--

00:56:11.790 --> 00:56:13.580
- So we got a picture--
- Right with what?

00:56:13.580 --> 00:56:15.480
- The two of us got a
picture with this woman.

00:56:15.480 --> 00:56:18.410
That's happened like five times.

00:56:18.410 --> 00:56:22.080
- Ironically was I not like
a block and a half away

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:24.250
and we had just split up
to go in different stores

00:56:24.250 --> 00:56:25.240
at that moment.

00:56:25.240 --> 00:56:26.960
- No that wasn't that day,
that was a different day.

00:56:26.960 --> 00:56:28.170
- 'Cause that happened then too.

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:30.220
No someone got a picture with
you and you didn't tell 'em

00:56:30.220 --> 00:56:32.170
that I was across the
street, you spared me.

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:35.520
- Yeah yeah, a lot of times
people are like where's Link?

00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:38.380
And 99% of the time I'm
like, well, I don't know.

00:56:38.380 --> 00:56:40.520
I don't have a geotag on him or whatever.

00:56:40.520 --> 00:56:43.250
- Right I have one on you
so I tell them exactly

00:56:43.250 --> 00:56:45.650
where you are, that's why
you get recognized so much.

00:56:45.650 --> 00:56:47.150
People are going after you.
- In that moment,

00:56:47.150 --> 00:56:49.400
you were literally on the
opposite street corner.

00:56:49.400 --> 00:56:50.670
- I was watching it happen.

00:56:50.670 --> 00:56:52.330
- Where's Link, I don't know.

00:56:52.330 --> 00:56:55.070
I currently see him over
your shoulder. (laughs)

00:56:55.070 --> 00:56:57.400
But I'm not gonna sick 'em on you.

00:56:57.400 --> 00:56:59.510
- Sometimes I like to, if
people are takin' a picture

00:56:59.510 --> 00:57:02.270
with you, I like to sneak
up at the last second

00:57:02.270 --> 00:57:05.850
and get in the photo, kinda
like how really famous people do

00:57:05.850 --> 00:57:08.280
when they're like in the Marvel universe

00:57:08.280 --> 00:57:11.068
and then they show up
and sneak in the photos.

00:57:11.068 --> 00:57:12.841
- Well and that, 'cause you
know what you were talking

00:57:12.841 --> 00:57:14.945
about with, I don't feel, again,

00:57:14.945 --> 00:57:17.610
I watched that documentary
about Bill Murray

00:57:19.010 --> 00:57:22.500
which by the way, I don't
know if I could recommend it

00:57:22.500 --> 00:57:24.515
because I was kind of
annoyed by the filmmaker

00:57:24.515 --> 00:57:27.060
and I just didn't like
the way the film was made

00:57:27.060 --> 00:57:28.330
but I really like Bill Murray

00:57:28.330 --> 00:57:30.780
and I really like these
stories about people.

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:32.360
- He shows up at places.
- He shows up

00:57:32.360 --> 00:57:34.310
and he does these things and he's Bill.

00:57:35.430 --> 00:57:40.360
We're not Bill Murray but I
also have this abiding sense

00:57:40.360 --> 00:57:44.440
that I could never do
that because I'm this guy

00:57:44.440 --> 00:57:45.273
from the internet.

00:57:45.273 --> 00:57:48.300
There may be some circles
that if I showed up,

00:57:48.300 --> 00:57:50.700
me and you showed up, it would
be a big deal for people,

00:57:50.700 --> 00:57:53.170
but for the majority of people,

00:57:53.170 --> 00:57:56.370
there's one person in the
group who knows who we are

00:57:56.370 --> 00:57:58.403
and then everyone else starts asking,

00:57:59.780 --> 00:58:00.990
who are those guys?

00:58:00.990 --> 00:58:03.890
And then people start asking us.

00:58:03.890 --> 00:58:05.131
- Oh that's the worst.

00:58:05.131 --> 00:58:06.810
- That happened in a
coffee shop the other day.

00:58:06.810 --> 00:58:08.520
We were down in Seal Beach

00:58:08.520 --> 00:58:10.917
where Locke had some basketball games

00:58:10.917 --> 00:58:13.810
and he had a game in the
afternoon and a game at night

00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:15.760
so we had all this time to
kill, we're walking around,

00:58:15.760 --> 00:58:17.220
go into this coffee shop.

00:58:17.220 --> 00:58:19.320
- All these seals to
kill, what did you say?

00:58:19.320 --> 00:58:20.350
- Time to kill.
- Okay good.

00:58:20.350 --> 00:58:21.940
- At Seal Beach.
- Okay, better.

00:58:21.940 --> 00:58:24.030
- Did not kill any seals.

00:58:24.030 --> 00:58:25.309
Didn't even see any.

00:58:25.309 --> 00:58:27.110
- Did you have a club?
- It's a misnomer.

00:58:27.110 --> 00:58:28.170
- They show up when you have a club.

00:58:28.170 --> 00:58:31.869
- I go into the coffee shop

00:58:31.869 --> 00:58:36.233
and the girl working the
register is like oh hey.

00:58:37.482 --> 00:58:39.083
A big fan of your show.

00:58:40.160 --> 00:58:42.620
And we had a pleasant interaction.

00:58:42.620 --> 00:58:43.520
- That's perfect.

00:58:43.520 --> 00:58:47.870
- And then an older woman
who was in line behind me,

00:58:47.870 --> 00:58:50.780
she was like, well what do you do?

00:58:50.780 --> 00:58:51.770
- Oh gosh.

00:58:51.770 --> 00:58:52.760
- This happens quite a bit.

00:58:52.760 --> 00:58:55.060
- I hate having to explain
what I do to people

00:58:55.060 --> 00:58:56.234
who don't actually care.

00:58:56.234 --> 00:58:57.850
- Well ever since...

00:59:00.290 --> 00:59:03.388
A few dozen times, I
was like, I got a show

00:59:03.388 --> 00:59:06.143
on the internet that, you know.

00:59:07.130 --> 00:59:09.210
Well what do you do on the show?

00:59:09.210 --> 00:59:12.057
That conversation, that annoys me, okay.

00:59:12.057 --> 00:59:15.270
And all the things that happen
with getting recognized,

00:59:15.270 --> 00:59:17.130
that is the thing that
annoys me the most is

00:59:17.130 --> 00:59:21.160
you're making me tell
you why someone that,

00:59:21.160 --> 00:59:26.020
so what I do now, whether
it's a child who recognizes me

00:59:26.020 --> 00:59:27.870
and the parents are like
why do they, I say--

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:29.720
- I feel like it's even
worse with the parents

00:59:29.720 --> 00:59:31.540
because they have a
relationship with the person

00:59:31.540 --> 00:59:34.230
who knows us so that could be an easy way

00:59:34.230 --> 00:59:35.190
to have a conversation.

00:59:35.190 --> 00:59:38.850
- It was worse when I lean
over, I turn to the girl

00:59:38.850 --> 00:59:41.260
who was waiting on me at the cash register

00:59:41.260 --> 00:59:43.932
and I said, "She'll tell
you all about it." (laughs)

00:59:43.932 --> 00:59:44.765
- Oh.

00:59:44.765 --> 00:59:46.830
- So that's what I do now,
it's like what do you?

00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:48.740
I say, "Well, ask her."

00:59:48.740 --> 00:59:51.290
I mean, I know that
I'm putting that person

00:59:51.290 --> 00:59:54.670
in a situation and I know
that I'm kinda seeming like

00:59:54.670 --> 00:59:57.530
a complete jerk now, I've told
you that the way I respond

00:59:57.530 --> 00:59:59.457
to these things but it happens,

00:59:59.457 --> 01:00:02.960
I just had these sort of back
pocket responses for people.

01:00:02.960 --> 01:00:04.670
I used to watch, why'd you stop?

01:00:04.670 --> 01:00:06.280
- May I make a suggestion?

01:00:06.280 --> 01:00:08.240
I think if you tweak your
language a little bit,

01:00:08.240 --> 01:00:10.010
it could be a little
more self-deprecating.

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:12.650
Like instead of saying, "Ask her."

01:00:12.650 --> 01:00:14.230
Even with the up at the end.

01:00:14.230 --> 01:00:15.550
- I say it nicely.
- Well I know

01:00:15.550 --> 01:00:16.630
you say it nicely.

01:00:16.630 --> 01:00:18.000
But you could go a step further

01:00:18.000 --> 01:00:20.067
and you could say something like,

01:00:20.067 --> 01:00:23.760
"I think it would be better
hearing it from her."

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:24.921
And then it's like, what?

01:00:24.921 --> 01:00:25.910
- I think the tone--
- He's embarrassed

01:00:25.910 --> 01:00:28.290
to talk about what he does,
he must be a porn star.

01:00:28.290 --> 01:00:29.770
- I think the tone of,
I say, you know what--

01:00:29.770 --> 01:00:31.080
- Then you got her.
- I say--

01:00:31.080 --> 01:00:32.130
- Then she's afraid to ask.

01:00:32.130 --> 01:00:33.930
- I say it in a joking way.

01:00:33.930 --> 01:00:35.800
In all those responses.
- I know, I know.

01:00:35.800 --> 01:00:38.650
- So I say, "She'll tell ya all about it."

01:00:38.650 --> 01:00:40.253
And then it usually just results in

01:00:40.253 --> 01:00:43.070
no one saying anything else
and I just get my coffee

01:00:43.070 --> 01:00:44.220
and start drinking it.

01:00:44.220 --> 01:00:47.170
- Yeah that's, I don't like that either.

01:00:47.170 --> 01:00:48.140
When the parent

01:00:49.696 --> 01:00:53.190
asking multiple questions to figure out

01:00:53.190 --> 01:00:55.693
why the kid likes us.

01:00:56.885 --> 01:00:57.740
I mean it's like you know--

01:00:57.740 --> 01:01:00.750
- Well it'd be one thing if
it was like I'm an actor.

01:01:00.750 --> 01:01:01.890
It's like--
- Right, right,

01:01:01.890 --> 01:01:02.723
it's hard to explain.

01:01:02.723 --> 01:01:03.700
- If I tell you I'm a YouTuber,

01:01:03.700 --> 01:01:04.960
you'll have a question about that,

01:01:04.960 --> 01:01:06.950
and then if I tell you
what I do on YouTube,

01:01:06.950 --> 01:01:08.350
you'll have a question about that.

01:01:08.350 --> 01:01:09.320
- Right.
- And then you'll just

01:01:09.320 --> 01:01:11.003
be like why's my child a fan of this?

01:01:11.003 --> 01:01:15.270
- It's the same principle
as if you do get a selfie

01:01:15.270 --> 01:01:19.140
with somebody at an amusement
park or crowded area,

01:01:19.140 --> 01:01:23.840
like a mall or something,
then there is a chance

01:01:23.840 --> 01:01:26.033
that a little line will form.

01:01:26.990 --> 01:01:30.270
Which brings me to, I think
the biggest sensitivity

01:01:30.270 --> 01:01:33.363
to this whole thing is
being out with our families.

01:01:34.523 --> 01:01:37.400
Because it really puts a wrench,

01:01:37.400 --> 01:01:42.110
we don't make public plans in
the family that involve adding

01:01:44.809 --> 01:01:49.809
10% margin to accommodate
for us being stopped,

01:01:51.050 --> 01:01:52.203
me being stopped.

01:01:53.460 --> 01:01:55.810
And then having to separate from me

01:01:55.810 --> 01:01:59.160
so that they won't be
in the photo, you know.

01:01:59.160 --> 01:02:02.530
And then so I think it's...

01:02:03.560 --> 01:02:06.663
it really tests their patience
and I fully understand that.

01:02:07.920 --> 01:02:08.753
Or do I?

01:02:09.790 --> 01:02:13.633
I was at, we went to the
Shazam movie premiere

01:02:13.633 --> 01:02:17.980
and it was thrilling.

01:02:17.980 --> 01:02:21.230
We did Instagram stories
like I had Shando with me

01:02:21.230 --> 01:02:25.486
and you in the Ferris wheel,
with my wife by the way, right?

01:02:25.486 --> 01:02:26.980
- No.

01:02:26.980 --> 01:02:28.600
I was with my--
- Were you?

01:02:28.600 --> 01:02:30.293
- Your wife and your daughter--

01:02:30.293 --> 01:02:32.630
- Were in the Ferris wheel.
- Different cart.

01:02:32.630 --> 01:02:34.820
- Okay and she's afraid
of heights so I wanted

01:02:34.820 --> 01:02:36.070
to watch her be in a Ferris wheel

01:02:36.070 --> 01:02:38.163
but I'm glad you weren't there.

01:02:39.060 --> 01:02:40.140
In such a tender moment.

01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:41.440
- Did she have a difficult time?

01:02:41.440 --> 01:02:43.940
- I think she did great,
she overcame some sort

01:02:43.940 --> 01:02:45.033
of Ferris wheel fear.

01:02:45.870 --> 01:02:48.790
They frickin' shut down
the Hollywood Boulevard

01:02:48.790 --> 01:02:50.570
and set up a whole carnival.

01:02:50.570 --> 01:02:54.600
And then it was just
invite only, you know,

01:02:54.600 --> 01:02:56.693
fortunately we got in on that.

01:02:58.020 --> 01:03:01.340
And we're walking around,
getting free fair food

01:03:01.340 --> 01:03:04.550
and all this stuff and
then Christy turns to Lily

01:03:04.550 --> 01:03:05.540
when I'm standing there, she's like,

01:03:05.540 --> 01:03:08.203
look it's Sterling K. Brown.

01:03:09.080 --> 01:03:11.600
One of the stars of This Is Us,

01:03:11.600 --> 01:03:15.235
the show that even
mentioning it makes you cry.

01:03:15.235 --> 01:03:16.068
(Rhett sniffles)

01:03:16.068 --> 01:03:18.300
They both love the show
and they bonded over it

01:03:18.300 --> 01:03:20.290
and I know who the guy is,
I haven't watched the show

01:03:20.290 --> 01:03:22.710
and of course we had the
other guy from This Is Us

01:03:22.710 --> 01:03:24.080
on Good Mythical Morning.

01:03:24.080 --> 01:03:26.030
- Chris.
- Chris Sullivan, absolutely.

01:03:26.030 --> 01:03:27.820
Anyway, Sterling has not been on our show.

01:03:27.820 --> 01:03:30.750
We had never met him but
when my wife and my daughter

01:03:30.750 --> 01:03:35.070
are talking about how much of a connection

01:03:35.070 --> 01:03:36.980
they make with the show and
they're like geeking out

01:03:36.980 --> 01:03:39.797
and I actually think that Christy,

01:03:39.797 --> 01:03:42.120
I'm gonna pull her under
the bus with me here,

01:03:42.120 --> 01:03:46.170
I do think she said, "Maybe
we can get a picture."

01:03:46.170 --> 01:03:47.423
He's sittin' right there.

01:03:49.170 --> 01:03:52.910
He and his son who was like grade school,

01:03:52.910 --> 01:03:56.050
were enjoying some fair food on a bench.

01:03:56.050 --> 01:03:58.563
We were standing there
whispering about him.

01:04:01.430 --> 01:04:04.083
I just was like I'll ask him for a photo.

01:04:05.726 --> 01:04:07.700
And they're like oh oh, okay.

01:04:07.700 --> 01:04:10.200
And I go up there and I'm
like, Sterling, how you doing?

01:04:10.200 --> 01:04:13.360
And I shook his hand,
I was like, I'm Link.

01:04:13.360 --> 01:04:17.130
I just wanna let you know,
boy, as I'm telling this,

01:04:17.130 --> 01:04:19.190
there's a lot of faux pas
here that we've discussed.

01:04:19.190 --> 01:04:20.460
The first thing I said was--

01:04:20.460 --> 01:04:23.933
- I used to be a fan.
- My wife and daughter

01:04:23.933 --> 01:04:27.320
are huge fans.

01:04:27.320 --> 01:04:29.233
Would you mind if I snapped a photo?

01:04:31.324 --> 01:04:33.112
So again, I was like--

01:04:33.112 --> 01:04:36.630
- I feel like, go on, but I
feel like that's different.

01:04:36.630 --> 01:04:38.263
Because they were there.

01:04:39.160 --> 01:04:40.270
- Yeah it is different.

01:04:40.270 --> 01:04:42.000
- And I'm not annoyed
by it, I'm just saying,

01:04:42.000 --> 01:04:44.080
it's just the case that
most people say that.

01:04:44.080 --> 01:04:48.990
- And Sterling K. Brown said--

01:04:48.990 --> 01:04:50.199
- No.

01:04:50.199 --> 01:04:55.199
- His face changed to
this apologetic, yet kind,

01:04:55.920 --> 01:04:59.863
I can make you cry at
will on television face.

01:05:00.850 --> 01:05:05.850
He said, "This is really
family time for me and my son,

01:05:06.023 --> 01:05:10.317
"so I'm gonna need to pass."

01:05:11.770 --> 01:05:13.370
I don't know if he said
I'm gonna need to pass

01:05:13.370 --> 01:05:17.140
but he said something
the most gracious way.

01:05:17.140 --> 01:05:19.230
I was like hmm, I'm gonna write that down.

01:05:19.230 --> 01:05:24.230
And then I walked away, totally,
I felt like a total jack

01:05:25.640 --> 01:05:27.680
for asking him for the photo.

01:05:27.680 --> 01:05:30.530
And then I turn around
and I yelled at Christy

01:05:30.530 --> 01:05:34.120
and Lily in front of everybody,
"Look what you made me do!"

01:05:34.120 --> 01:05:37.723
No I didn't, I was like,
he's having family time.

01:05:38.820 --> 01:05:41.610
And I was like, and then
it clicked for them too

01:05:41.610 --> 01:05:44.850
and they're like, all three
of us, it clicked in our minds

01:05:44.850 --> 01:05:48.520
I think at that moment, it's like wait,

01:05:48.520 --> 01:05:50.460
I bet we could have that too.

01:05:50.460 --> 01:05:52.350
You know, it was like the tables--

01:05:52.350 --> 01:05:53.290
- We could have family time.

01:05:53.290 --> 01:05:57.710
- The tables turned where
it was like what if,

01:05:57.710 --> 01:06:00.883
learning from my mentor,
Sterling K. Brown,

01:06:02.814 --> 01:06:06.480
I adopted a similar
mantra when it came to,

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:09.260
I'm out in public but
I'm having special time

01:06:09.260 --> 01:06:12.320
with my wife or my kids.

01:06:12.320 --> 01:06:13.360
- This is a difficult one.

01:06:13.360 --> 01:06:16.784
- I respected him totally and I felt--

01:06:16.784 --> 01:06:20.900
- I have no judgment of him, by the way.

01:06:20.900 --> 01:06:22.480
- I felt bad for asking him.

01:06:22.480 --> 01:06:23.590
I felt like--
- Did you talk to him

01:06:23.590 --> 01:06:24.820
the other night at that thing?

01:06:24.820 --> 01:06:28.230
He did not recognize, we went
to a party and he was there

01:06:28.230 --> 01:06:30.830
and we might tell more of the
details of that party later

01:06:30.830 --> 01:06:31.663
so I wanna save it.

01:06:31.663 --> 01:06:32.810
- Okay.
- Except for the fact

01:06:32.810 --> 01:06:35.490
that I shook his hand
and I looked in his eyes

01:06:35.490 --> 01:06:37.990
and I was like, is he gonna
recognize me as that guy?

01:06:37.990 --> 01:06:40.430
- No, I'm sure he wouldn't.
- He didn't.

01:06:40.430 --> 01:06:42.093
Even though it was me and Christy there.

01:06:42.093 --> 01:06:45.610
- This is interesting because
okay, at that same thing--

01:06:45.610 --> 01:06:47.843
- 'Cause I wanna talk
about these boundaries,

01:06:47.843 --> 01:06:48.676
that's what I'm getting at.

01:06:48.676 --> 01:06:50.253
- 'Cause at the same premiere,

01:06:52.490 --> 01:06:56.670
a number of people, 10,
I don't know, came up--

01:06:56.670 --> 01:06:57.503
- And asked to get pictures with us.

01:06:57.503 --> 01:07:00.010
- And got pictures with
us and some people,

01:07:00.010 --> 01:07:03.390
like one of 'em was, it
was a mom, she came up

01:07:03.390 --> 01:07:07.610
and she was like, my son is a huge fan.

01:07:07.610 --> 01:07:10.680
And he was there and turns out,

01:07:10.680 --> 01:07:12.230
he was in the freakin' movie.

01:07:12.230 --> 01:07:14.718
He was one of the kids
in the family in Shazam.

01:07:14.718 --> 01:07:19.718
And we, and that, I think
two things are going on.

01:07:20.160 --> 01:07:23.950
Number one, we don't see
ourselves on the level

01:07:23.950 --> 01:07:26.310
of Sterling K. Brown, right,
I know there are some people

01:07:26.310 --> 01:07:27.620
who are like that's ridiculous.

01:07:27.620 --> 01:07:30.680
There's no difference
between what you do and,

01:07:30.680 --> 01:07:34.150
well I'm just telling you, I
see him as a real celebrity

01:07:34.150 --> 01:07:37.330
and I see myself as a
YouTuber and I know that's,

01:07:37.330 --> 01:07:40.110
I'm selling the whole
platform short by saying that

01:07:40.110 --> 01:07:43.480
but just mentally, because
anybody can make a YouTube video.

01:07:43.480 --> 01:07:45.060
Not anybody can just go and say,

01:07:45.060 --> 01:07:47.510
I'm on an episode of This Is Us.

01:07:47.510 --> 01:07:49.280
You can make your own show on YouTube

01:07:49.280 --> 01:07:50.547
just as easily as we can.

01:07:51.700 --> 01:07:53.770
So it just feels like it's--

01:07:53.770 --> 01:07:55.060
- Or can you?

01:07:55.060 --> 01:07:58.130
- It feels like there's a
different thing going on.

01:07:58.130 --> 01:08:02.272
Maybe he has experienced, well, if I come,

01:08:02.272 --> 01:08:04.555
I'm bringing my kid to this thing

01:08:04.555 --> 01:08:07.570
and if I get a picture with one person,

01:08:07.570 --> 01:08:09.810
it would lead to a picture
with, and all of a sudden,

01:08:09.810 --> 01:08:12.320
this is about me and not about my son.

01:08:12.320 --> 01:08:14.283
So I completely understand that.

01:08:16.090 --> 01:08:20.070
But for me when we were
at the Shazam premiere,

01:08:20.070 --> 01:08:24.540
I always feel like we shouldn't
be there, imposter syndrome.

01:08:24.540 --> 01:08:28.640
I'm like how did we, we're just
two guys from North Carolina

01:08:28.640 --> 01:08:31.155
who just kinda got lucky and nobody knows

01:08:31.155 --> 01:08:34.960
that we're just a couple of
dumb rednecks that just know

01:08:34.960 --> 01:08:36.300
how to put hair product in.

01:08:36.300 --> 01:08:38.470
- And maybe if Sterling K. Brown sees us

01:08:38.470 --> 01:08:41.400
get a picture with somebody,
then something will,

01:08:41.400 --> 01:08:42.660
maybe we can be friends with him.

01:08:42.660 --> 01:08:46.003
- Exactly, and so when
you're in this mode,

01:08:47.000 --> 01:08:49.547
yeah, I got to admit, it's like oh,

01:08:49.547 --> 01:08:51.530
the other real celebrities here.

01:08:51.530 --> 01:08:53.352
If somebody wants a picture with me,

01:08:53.352 --> 01:08:57.120
there's that dynamic and so,

01:08:57.120 --> 01:09:01.078
but Sterling K. Brown is not
thinking about it like that.

01:09:01.078 --> 01:09:03.300
He isn't trying to impress anybody.

01:09:03.300 --> 01:09:05.410
- Right.
- And so,

01:09:05.410 --> 01:09:09.010
I guess when you get to this centered,

01:09:09.010 --> 01:09:13.670
balanced place, you can say things like

01:09:13.670 --> 01:09:16.550
I'm having family time right
now so I'm gonna have to pass.

01:09:16.550 --> 01:09:18.260
And you say that without apology.

01:09:18.260 --> 01:09:21.410
So are you now saying that
that is what you're gonna say

01:09:21.410 --> 01:09:22.490
when you're with your family?

01:09:22.490 --> 01:09:24.183
- I think I'm going to,

01:09:25.870 --> 01:09:28.010
when I go into certain situations.

01:09:28.010 --> 01:09:32.350
If it's a special event
that I'm bringing one of

01:09:32.350 --> 01:09:34.980
my kids to because they
are particularly excited

01:09:34.980 --> 01:09:36.940
about that and it is
about, hey I'm bringing you

01:09:36.940 --> 01:09:38.360
to this thing, we're talking about it,

01:09:38.360 --> 01:09:40.190
and there's a buildup to
it and then we get there

01:09:40.190 --> 01:09:44.946
and I'm spending 10% of my
time taking photos with people,

01:09:44.946 --> 01:09:49.946
I think that that sends,
it's a missed opportunity

01:09:50.000 --> 01:09:54.980
to send a message to that child

01:09:54.980 --> 01:09:58.040
that you're the priority
and this is about you,

01:09:58.040 --> 01:09:59.080
this is not about me.

01:09:59.080 --> 01:10:02.200
I think we've just broken
down the psychology

01:10:02.200 --> 01:10:06.140
of what selfies could mean to
us in a situation like that.

01:10:06.140 --> 01:10:09.190
And I think that so I'm
not saying across the board

01:10:09.190 --> 01:10:13.200
but I'm saying I'm gonna
choose, I'm gonna try to choose

01:10:15.180 --> 01:10:17.590
going into events, like a mindset of

01:10:20.490 --> 01:10:22.413
doing that, like especially

01:10:22.413 --> 01:10:26.050
if it's an enclosed
space where there's lots

01:10:26.050 --> 01:10:27.629
of people that could be fans.

01:10:27.629 --> 01:10:29.750
- I think the environment is a big deal.

01:10:29.750 --> 01:10:30.800
If you're walking down the street--

01:10:30.800 --> 01:10:32.440
- Leads to another, leads to another.

01:10:32.440 --> 01:10:34.570
- If you're just walking down
the street with your family,

01:10:34.570 --> 01:10:37.170
having family time, and
somebody's like hey,

01:10:37.170 --> 01:10:38.480
can I get a picture?

01:10:38.480 --> 01:10:42.227
That's a 10 second
interaction or whatever.

01:10:42.227 --> 01:10:44.440
If they wanna start talking
and they have a bunch

01:10:44.440 --> 01:10:46.290
of questions, at that point may be like,

01:10:46.290 --> 01:10:49.297
I'm sorry, I'm with my family now.

01:10:49.297 --> 01:10:51.420
People are still gonna think
you're a jerk for doing it,

01:10:51.420 --> 01:10:52.980
just so you know, 'cause
people are gonna be like,

01:10:52.980 --> 01:10:55.400
why can't he just give me a minute?

01:10:55.400 --> 01:10:57.400
That's just the way people are.

01:10:57.400 --> 01:11:00.970
Like you actually took a
very mature perspective

01:11:00.970 --> 01:11:03.290
on the way that you responded
to Sterling K. Brown

01:11:03.290 --> 01:11:07.900
and you're only able to do
that because you can relate.

01:11:07.900 --> 01:11:10.680
But if somebody couldn't
relate, they would just be like

01:11:10.680 --> 01:11:11.910
he's kind of a jerk for doing that.

01:11:11.910 --> 01:11:13.660
They wouldn't go to the
next step and be like,

01:11:13.660 --> 01:11:16.750
oh that's sweet that he's
doing that for his kid.

01:11:16.750 --> 01:11:18.450
Because there's dynamics that people

01:11:18.450 --> 01:11:20.370
don't understand going on.

01:11:20.370 --> 01:11:23.060
So I do think that the
environment is really important.

01:11:23.060 --> 01:11:24.980
If I'm just out in the middle of somewhere

01:11:24.980 --> 01:11:28.100
where it's not like all of
a sudden a crowd could form

01:11:28.100 --> 01:11:32.397
or somebody else, I'm
gonna say, "Okay sure.

01:11:32.397 --> 01:11:34.240
"Let's do a quick photo or whatever."

01:11:34.240 --> 01:11:36.610
But I do think that if it's,

01:11:36.610 --> 01:11:40.210
amusement parks are the supreme situation

01:11:40.210 --> 01:11:42.428
because you're there for your family

01:11:42.428 --> 01:11:47.070
to have a good time and one
recognition and one picture

01:11:47.070 --> 01:11:49.363
leads to more and then it becomes,

01:11:50.558 --> 01:11:54.130
the first time I went to
Disneyland with my kids

01:11:54.130 --> 01:11:56.530
right when people were
starting to recognize us,

01:11:58.080 --> 01:11:59.950
I ruined their day.

01:11:59.950 --> 01:12:01.430
It ruined their day.

01:12:01.430 --> 01:12:03.150
- Right.
- 'Cause I was trying

01:12:03.150 --> 01:12:06.610
to be nice and I'm like oh,
I'm the approachable guy

01:12:06.610 --> 01:12:09.990
from YouTube, yeah of course
you can get a picture with me.

01:12:09.990 --> 01:12:13.940
And that turned into I've gotta get a hat.

01:12:13.940 --> 01:12:15.470
I've gotta get a hat and sunglasses

01:12:15.470 --> 01:12:18.070
and then I always felt
like one of those douches

01:12:18.070 --> 01:12:21.150
walking around with a hat
and, I'm wearing a hat

01:12:21.150 --> 01:12:22.310
and sunglasses at Disneyland

01:12:22.310 --> 01:12:23.941
so you won't recognize me douche.

01:12:23.941 --> 01:12:24.830
- Excuse me sir, can I
get a picture with you

01:12:24.830 --> 01:12:27.184
because I've never seen
someone so douche-like.

01:12:27.184 --> 01:12:28.584
(Rhett chuckles)

01:12:28.584 --> 01:12:30.050
Your douche level is so high.

01:12:30.050 --> 01:12:33.350
- I ruined my family's day so I do think

01:12:33.350 --> 01:12:35.200
that you kinda have to
evaluate the environment

01:12:35.200 --> 01:12:39.080
and then you have to have,
I'm sure he said that line,

01:12:39.080 --> 01:12:40.855
I'm sorry.

01:12:40.855 --> 01:12:43.560
I'm having family time right
now so I'm gonna have to pass.

01:12:43.560 --> 01:12:46.080
He's worked on that line.
- Yeah.

01:12:46.080 --> 01:12:50.190
- He's made it as
un-disarming as possible.

01:12:50.190 --> 01:12:52.440
- Sterling K. disarmament.

01:12:52.440 --> 01:12:54.590
That's what I'm gonna
be doing from now on.

01:12:55.930 --> 01:12:57.880
Yeah I think that's helpful, I think...

01:13:00.780 --> 01:13:03.050
I think that's a good
stopping point, you know.

01:13:03.050 --> 01:13:05.590
Food for thought, I think this
has been beneficial for us.

01:13:05.590 --> 01:13:07.540
I don't know what it's done for you

01:13:07.540 --> 01:13:08.930
but thanks for hanging in there.

01:13:08.930 --> 01:13:12.300
- Hopefully it hasn't made
you incredibly nervous

01:13:12.300 --> 01:13:14.250
about if you see us in public.

01:13:14.250 --> 01:13:15.510
I don't know what to do now

01:13:15.510 --> 01:13:17.030
that they've talked about it like this.

01:13:17.030 --> 01:13:19.383
Just be yourself, just be yourself.

01:13:20.230 --> 01:13:23.690
Be yourself and evaluate the environment.

01:13:23.690 --> 01:13:25.080
We'll be doing the same thing.

01:13:25.080 --> 01:13:29.377
- Yeah, but it's on us
to set the boundary.

01:13:33.560 --> 01:13:35.470
Leave that to us.

01:13:35.470 --> 01:13:37.040
We will crush you.

01:13:37.040 --> 01:13:37.890
No we won't.

01:13:37.890 --> 01:13:38.940
- You have a rec for us?

01:13:38.940 --> 01:13:42.020
- Oh yeah, as a reward
for hanging on so long,

01:13:42.020 --> 01:13:44.066
give you a recommendation.

